        "  padding: 0.4rem 1rem !important;\n",
        "}\n",
        "/* Hover/focus state: keep text black, light grey background for feedback */\n",
        ".quarto-code-tools-button + .dropdown-menu .dropdown-item:hover,\n",
        ".quarto-code-tools-button + .dropdown-menu .dropdown-item:focus,\n",
        "#quarto-code-tools-menu + .dropdown-menu .dropdown-item:hover,\n",
        "#quarto-code-tools-menu + .dropdown-menu .dropdown-item:focus,\n",
        ".code-tools-button + .dropdown-menu .dropdown-item:hover,\n",
        ".code-tools-button + .dropdown-menu .dropdown-item:focus,\n",
        "ul.dropdown-menu[aria-labelledby*=\"code-tools\"] .dropdown-item:hover,\n",
        "ul.dropdown-menu[aria-labelledby*=\"code-tools\"] .dropdown-item:focus,\n",
        ".dropdown-menu.show .dropdown-item:hover,\n",
        ".dropdown-menu.show .dropdown-item:focus {\n",
        "  color: #000000 !important;\n",
        "  background-color: #F1F5F9 !important;\n",
        "}\n",
        "</style>\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "## 1. Research question\n",
        "\n",
        "> **Is Australia's fire-affected environment doing well?**\n",
        "\n",
        "The honest answer this analysis lands on is: **it depends entirely on which\n",
        "layer of \"the environment\" you look at, and that dependence is getting\n",
        "worse.** The central, load-bearing finding is this:\n",
        "\n",
        "> **The gap between vegetation recovery and wildlife recovery after a major\n",
        "> fire is not fixed -- it widens as post-fire heat exposure increases\n",
        "> (Spearman rho = 0.343, p = 0.033; consistent in direction across five\n",
        "> independent tests, three of which reach p < 0.05).** As the climate keeps\n",
        "> warming, the reassuring \"it's green again\" signal from satellite NDVI\n",
        "> becomes a progressively worse proxy for \"the habitat is fine.\" Anyone\n",
        "> reading only vegetation greenness -- which is the cheapest, most visible,\n",
        "> most commonly reported recovery metric -- would systematically underestimate\n",
        "> how bad conditions are for the animals living there, and that blind spot\n",
        "> compounds with every degree of warming rather than staying constant.\n",
        "\n",
        "Everything below builds and stress-tests that one claim: the dataset, the\n",
        "event-level construction, an interactive map of where it shows up, the\n",
        "statistical tests, and finally a direct answer to the research question\n",
        "built on top of it.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 2. Setup"
      ],
      "id": "574972e3"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import os\n",
        "import json\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "from scipy import stats\n",
        "import statsmodels.formula.api as smf\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "import geopandas as gpd\n",
        "import plotly.express as px\n",
        "\n",
        "DATA_DIR = os.path.join(os.getcwd(), \"..\", \"data\")  # report lives in reports/, data lives in ../data\n",
        "pd.set_option(\"display.width\", 140)"
      ],
      "id": "918fcd8d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 3. Data and event-level construction\n",
        "\n",
        "Four datasets, all already built/cached by the companion notebooks in this\n",
        "folder (`fire_history_eda.ipynb`, `fire_heat_recovery_analysis.qmd`):\n",
        "\n",
        "| Dataset | Role |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| NSW/QLD/VIC fire history shapefiles | major fire events (>= 1,000 ha) and severity, per LGA-division-year |\n",
        "| `AusENDVI-clim_MCD43A4...nc` | vegetation recovery signal (NDVI) |\n",
        "| `animal_sightings.csv` | koala / greater glider / yellow-bellied glider presence |\n",
        "| ERA5-Land daily max temperature | post-fire heat exposure, relative to each division's own baseline |\n",
        "\n",
        "The analysis unit is the **individual major fire event** (division x\n",
        "fire-year, fire >= 1,000 ha) -- not the whole division. Vegetation and\n",
        "wildlife recovery are both computed as a Relative Recovery Indicator (RRI,\n",
        "adapted from Frazier et al., 2018 -- see Section 11), which requires a\n",
        "measurable dip in the fire year itself; that requirement is easy for NDVI\n",
        "(166 of 184 candidate events keep a valid ratio) but more demanding for\n",
        "sparse citizen-science sightings data (39 events, across 23 divisions,\n",
        "keep a valid ratio) -- see Section 9 for what that does to statistical\n",
        "power."
      ],
      "id": "96b747d7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "ndvi = pd.read_csv(os.path.join(DATA_DIR, \"ndvi_annual_by_division.csv\")).set_index([\"ADM1_NAME\", \"ADM2_NAME\"])\n",
        "ndvi.columns = ndvi.columns.astype(int)\n",
        "\n",
        "fire_events = pd.read_csv(os.path.join(DATA_DIR, \"fire_events_by_division_year.csv\"))\n",
        "major = fire_events[fire_events.major_fires > 0].copy()\n",
        "\n",
        "sightings = pd.read_csv(os.path.join(DATA_DIR, \"animal_sightings_by_division_year.csv\"))\n",
        "div_year = sightings.groupby([\"ADM1_NAME\", \"ADM2_NAME\", \"year\"])[\"sightings\"].sum().reset_index()\n",
        "total_per_year = div_year.groupby(\"year\")[\"sightings\"].sum().rename(\"total_sightings_all_divs\")\n",
        "div_year = div_year.merge(total_per_year, on=\"year\")\n",
        "div_year[\"share\"] = div_year[\"sightings\"] / div_year[\"total_sightings_all_divs\"]\n",
        "share_piv = div_year.pivot_table(index=[\"ADM1_NAME\", \"ADM2_NAME\"], columns=\"year\", values=\"share\")\n",
        "total_sightings_per_div = sightings.groupby([\"ADM1_NAME\", \"ADM2_NAME\"])[\"sightings\"].sum()\n",
        "\n",
        "heat = pd.read_csv(os.path.join(DATA_DIR, \"heat_stress_by_division_year.csv\"))\n",
        "heat_piv = heat.pivot_table(index=[\"ADM1_NAME\", \"ADM2_NAME\"], columns=\"year\", values=\"hot_days_ge35\")\n",
        "heat_baseline_mean = heat_piv.mean(axis=1)\n",
        "\n",
        "PRE_YEARS, HEAT_POST_LAG, RECOVERY_POST_LAGS, SMOOTH = 2, 3, [3, 4, 5], 1e-6\n",
        "\n",
        "records = []\n",
        "for _, r in major.iterrows():\n",
        "    state, div, year = r[\"ADM1_NAME\"], r[\"ADM2_NAME\"], int(r[\"year\"])\n",
        "    key = (state, div)\n",
        "    if key not in ndvi.index or key not in heat_piv.index:\n",
        "        continue\n",
        "    ndvi_row, heat_row = ndvi.loc[key], heat_piv.loc[key]\n",
        "    pre_years = [year - k for k in range(1, PRE_YEARS + 1)]\n",
        "    heat_post_years = [year + k for k in range(0, HEAT_POST_LAG + 1)]\n",
        "    recovery_post_years = [year + k for k in RECOVERY_POST_LAGS]\n",
        "\n",
        "    # Relative Recovery Indicator (RRI), adapted from Frazier et al. (2018):\n",
        "    # RRI = ARI / disturbance magnitude. Disturbance magnitude = pre-fire\n",
        "    # baseline minus the fire-year (Y0) value; ARI = best post-fire value\n",
        "    # observed minus Y0. The paper uses a fixed Y+4/Y+5 window, but NDVI here\n",
        "    # ends in 2022, so a fixed Y+4/Y+5 window would drop every fire from 2019\n",
        "    # onward (including the Black Summer cohort); instead we take the best\n",
        "    # available of Y+3/Y+4/Y+5.\n",
        "    pre_ndvi = [v for v in (ndvi_row.get(y) for y in pre_years) if pd.notna(v)]\n",
        "    y0_ndvi = ndvi_row.get(year)\n",
        "    post_ndvi = [v for v in (ndvi_row.get(y) for y in recovery_post_years) if pd.notna(v)]\n",
        "    if not pre_ndvi or pd.isna(y0_ndvi) or not post_ndvi:\n",
        "        continue\n",
        "    ndvi_baseline = np.mean(pre_ndvi)\n",
        "    ndvi_disturbance = ndvi_baseline - y0_ndvi\n",
        "    if ndvi_disturbance <= 0:\n",
        "        continue\n",
        "    ndvi_ratio = (max(post_ndvi) - y0_ndvi) / ndvi_disturbance\n",
        "\n",
        "    sight_ratio = np.nan\n",
        "    if key in share_piv.index:\n",
        "        srow = share_piv.loc[key]\n",
        "        pre_share = [v for v in (srow.get(y) for y in pre_years) if pd.notna(v)]\n",
        "        y0_share = srow.get(year)\n",
        "        post_share = [v for v in (srow.get(y) for y in recovery_post_years) if pd.notna(v)]\n",
        "        if pre_share and pd.notna(y0_share) and post_share:\n",
        "            share_baseline = np.mean(pre_share) + SMOOTH\n",
        "            y0_share_s = y0_share + SMOOTH\n",
        "            share_disturbance = share_baseline - y0_share_s\n",
        "            if share_disturbance > 0:\n",
        "                sight_ratio = (max(post_share) + SMOOTH - y0_share_s) / share_disturbance\n",
        "\n",
        "    post_heat = [v for v in (heat_row.get(y) for y in heat_post_years) if pd.notna(v)]\n",
        "    if not post_heat:\n",
        "        continue\n",
        "    heat_post_mean = np.mean(post_heat)\n",
        "    heat_anomaly = heat_post_mean - heat_baseline_mean.get(key, np.nan)\n",
        "\n",
        "    records.append({\n",
        "        \"ADM1_NAME\": state, \"ADM2_NAME\": div, \"fire_year\": year,\n",
        "        \"max_area_ha\": r[\"max_area_ha\"], \"major_fires\": r[\"major_fires\"],\n",
        "        \"ndvi_recovery_ratio\": ndvi_ratio, \"sightings_recovery_ratio\": sight_ratio,\n",
        "        \"heat_anomaly_postfire\": heat_anomaly,\n",
        "        \"division_total_sightings\": total_sightings_per_div.get(key, 0),\n",
        "    })\n",
        "\n",
        "events_all = pd.DataFrame(records)\n",
        "# RRI can be zero/negative (best post-fire value never exceeds the fire-year\n",
        "# value); those events can't be log-transformed and are dropped.\n",
        "events_all = events_all[events_all[\"ndvi_recovery_ratio\"] > 0].copy()\n",
        "events_all[\"log_ndvi_ratio\"] = np.log(events_all[\"ndvi_recovery_ratio\"])\n",
        "events_all[\"log_severity\"] = np.log(events_all[\"max_area_ha\"])\n",
        "\n",
        "HABITAT_MIN_SIGHTINGS = 200\n",
        "events = events_all[(events_all[\"division_total_sightings\"] >= HABITAT_MIN_SIGHTINGS)\n",
        "                     & events_all[\"sightings_recovery_ratio\"].notna()\n",
        "                     & (events_all[\"sightings_recovery_ratio\"] > 0)].copy()\n",
        "events[\"log_sight_ratio\"] = np.log(events[\"sightings_recovery_ratio\"])\n",
        "events[\"recovery_gap\"] = events[\"log_ndvi_ratio\"] - events[\"log_sight_ratio\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"{len(events_all)} major-fire events total; {len(events)} events with a valid recovery \"\n",
        "      f\"gap across {events['ADM2_NAME'].nunique()} divisions with real koala/glider presence \"\n",
        "      f\"(>= {HABITAT_MIN_SIGHTINGS} sightings on record)\")"
      ],
      "id": "5e542f7c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**NDVI/sightings recovery ratio** = RRI = (best post-fire value observed -\n",
        "fire-year value) / (pre-fire baseline - fire-year value) -- i.e. how much of\n",
        "the fire-year dip was recovered, not a plain year+3/baseline ratio. **Recovery\n",
        "gap** = log(NDVI recovery ratio) - log(sightings recovery ratio). Zero means\n",
        "vegetation and wildlife recovered by the same proportional amount relative to\n",
        "how hard each was hit; positive means vegetation recovered *relatively\n",
        "better* than wildlife did.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 4. EDA"
      ],
      "id": "48914c99"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "events[[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\", \"ndvi_recovery_ratio\", \"sightings_recovery_ratio\", \"recovery_gap\"]].describe()"
      ],
      "id": "b1f5b030",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(15, 4))\n",
        "\n",
        "axes[0].hist(events[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"], bins=30, color=\"steelblue\")\n",
        "axes[0].set_title(\"Post-Fire Heat Anomaly\")\n",
        "axes[0].set_xlabel(\"Extra hot days/yr (>=35C) vs. division's own baseline\")\n",
        "axes[0].set_ylabel(\"Number of fire events\")\n",
        "\n",
        "axes[1].hist(events[\"ndvi_recovery_ratio\"], bins=30, color=\"seagreen\")\n",
        "axes[1].axvline(1.0, color=\"grey\", ls=\"--\")\n",
        "axes[1].set_title(\"Vegetation (NDVI) Recovery Ratio (RRI)\")\n",
        "axes[1].set_xlabel(\"NDVI RRI (1.0 = fully recovered relative to the fire-year dip)\")\n",
        "axes[1].set_ylabel(\"Number of fire events\")\n",
        "\n",
        "axes[2].hist(events[\"recovery_gap\"], bins=30, color=\"firebrick\")\n",
        "axes[2].axvline(0, color=\"grey\", ls=\"--\")\n",
        "axes[2].set_title(\"Recovery Gap Distribution\")\n",
        "axes[2].set_xlabel(\"Recovery gap: log(NDVI ratio) - log(sightings ratio)\")\n",
        "axes[2].set_ylabel(\"Number of fire events\")\n",
        "\n",
        "fig.suptitle(\"Figure 1. Distributions of heat exposure, vegetation recovery, and the recovery gap \"\n",
        "             f\"({len(events)} major fire events)\", y=1.04)\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "cc4a597a",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The gap distribution is centred close to zero with a long right tail --\n",
        "most events show roughly balanced recovery, but a meaningful share show\n",
        "vegetation recovering much better than wildlife. The question is *what\n",
        "predicts which side of zero an event lands on.*\n",
        "\n",
        "## 5. Interactive map: where does the gap show up?\n",
        "\n",
        "Each fire-prone division's **mean recovery gap** across all its major-fire\n",
        "events, 1982-2022. Red = vegetation recovers substantially better than\n",
        "wildlife (the \"greenwashing risk\" divisions); blue = the two track each\n",
        "other. Hover any division for its heat exposure and both recovery ratios."
      ],
      "id": "06d34092"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "div_agg = events.groupby([\"ADM1_NAME\", \"ADM2_NAME\"]).agg(\n",
        "    n_events=(\"recovery_gap\", \"size\"),\n",
        "    mean_recovery_gap=(\"recovery_gap\", \"mean\"),\n",
        "    mean_heat_anomaly=(\"heat_anomaly_postfire\", \"mean\"),\n",
        "    mean_ndvi_ratio=(\"ndvi_recovery_ratio\", \"mean\"),\n",
        "    mean_sightings_ratio=(\"sightings_recovery_ratio\", \"mean\"),\n",
        ").reset_index()\n",
        "\n",
        "admin2 = gpd.read_file(os.path.join(DATA_DIR, \"au_admin2_boundaries.geojson\"))[[\"ADM1_NAME\", \"ADM2_NAME\", \"geometry\"]]\n",
        "geo = admin2.merge(div_agg[[\"ADM2_NAME\"]], on=\"ADM2_NAME\", how=\"inner\").reset_index(drop=True)\n",
        "geo[\"geometry\"] = geo.geometry.simplify(0.01, preserve_topology=True)  # keeps the embedded map lightweight\n",
        "geojson = json.loads(geo.to_json())\n",
        "\n",
        "div_agg_labelled = div_agg.rename(columns={\n",
        "    \"ADM1_NAME\": \"State\",\n",
        "    \"mean_recovery_gap\": \"Mean recovery gap\",\n",
        "    \"mean_heat_anomaly\": \"Mean post-fire heat anomaly (extra hot days/yr)\",\n",
        "    \"mean_ndvi_ratio\": \"Mean NDVI recovery ratio\",\n",
        "    \"mean_sightings_ratio\": \"Mean sightings recovery ratio\",\n",
        "    \"n_events\": \"Number of major fire events\",\n",
        "})\n",
        "\n",
        "fig_map = px.choropleth_map(\n",
        "    div_agg_labelled,\n",
        "    geojson=geojson,\n",
        "    locations=\"ADM2_NAME\",\n",
        "    featureidkey=\"properties.ADM2_NAME\",\n",
        "    color=\"Mean recovery gap\",\n",
        "    color_continuous_scale=\"RdBu_r\",\n",
        "    color_continuous_midpoint=0,\n",
        "    hover_name=\"ADM2_NAME\",\n",
        "    hover_data={\n",
        "        \"State\": True,\n",
        "        \"Mean recovery gap\": \":.2f\",\n",
        "        \"Mean post-fire heat anomaly (extra hot days/yr)\": \":.1f\",\n",
        "        \"Mean NDVI recovery ratio\": \":.2f\",\n",
        "        \"Mean sightings recovery ratio\": \":.2f\",\n",
        "        \"Number of major fire events\": True,\n",
        "    },\n",
        "    map_style=\"carto-positron\",\n",
        "    zoom=3.2,\n",
        "    center={\"lat\": -28, \"lon\": 145},\n",
        "    opacity=0.75,\n",
        "    height=650,\n",
        ")\n",
        "fig_map.update_layout(\n",
        "    title=dict(\n",
        "        text=\"Figure 2. Mean Post-Fire Recovery Gap by Fire-Prone Division<br>\"\n",
        "             \"<sup>Red = vegetation recovers better than wildlife (widening blind-spot risk); Blue = the two track together</sup>\",\n",
        "        x=0.02, y=0.98,\n",
        "    ),\n",
        "    margin=dict(l=0, r=0, t=70, b=0),\n",
        "    coloraxis_colorbar=dict(title=\"Mean recovery gap<br>(+ = vegetation<br>outpaces wildlife)\"),\n",
        ")\n",
        "fig_map"
      ],
      "id": "98ef0a44",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Divisions with a high mean gap tend to be inland/western-fringe fire-prone\n",
        "LGAs -- the same places that also run hotter post-fire heat anomalies, which\n",
        "is exactly the pattern the statistical tests below confirm formally.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 6. Visual evidence\n",
        "\n",
        "### 6.1 Diverging recovery lines\n",
        "\n",
        "The single clearest picture of the claim: plot both recovery ratios (log\n",
        "scale, 0 = back to baseline) against post-fire heat exposure and fit a trend\n",
        "line to each."
      ],
      "id": "16a6f087"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 5.5))\n",
        "xs = np.linspace(events[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"].min(), events[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"].max(), 50)\n",
        "z_ndvi = np.polyfit(events[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"], events[\"log_ndvi_ratio\"], 1)\n",
        "z_sight = np.polyfit(events[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"], events[\"log_sight_ratio\"], 1)\n",
        "\n",
        "ax.scatter(events[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"], events[\"log_ndvi_ratio\"], alpha=0.12, s=12, color=\"seagreen\")\n",
        "ax.scatter(events[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"], events[\"log_sight_ratio\"], alpha=0.12, s=12, color=\"darkorange\")\n",
        "ax.plot(xs, np.polyval(z_ndvi, xs), color=\"seagreen\", lw=3, label=\"NDVI recovery (vegetation)\")\n",
        "ax.plot(xs, np.polyval(z_sight, xs), color=\"darkorange\", lw=3, label=\"Sightings recovery (wildlife)\")\n",
        "ax.axhline(0, color=\"grey\", lw=0.7, ls=\"--\")\n",
        "ax.set_xlabel(\"Post-fire heat anomaly: extra hot days/yr (>=35C) vs. division's own baseline\")\n",
        "ax.set_ylabel(\"Log recovery ratio (0 = fully recovered to pre-fire baseline)\")\n",
        "ax.set_title(\"Figure 3. Vegetation Recovers Regardless of Heat -- Wildlife Does Not\")\n",
        "ax.legend(title=\"Recovery trend line\", loc=\"upper right\")\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "59b6479f",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The vegetation line is nearly flat across the entire heat range. The\n",
        "wildlife line starts *above* vegetation at low heat exposure (sightings\n",
        "often overshoot their pre-fire baseline when conditions are mild) and drops\n",
        "steadily as heat exposure rises, crossing below vegetation and continuing\n",
        "down. The vertical distance between the two lines *is* the recovery gap --\n",
        "and it visibly grows left to right.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 6.2 The gap by heat tercile"
      ],
      "id": "a742dcd9"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "events[\"heat_tercile\"] = pd.qcut(events[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"], 3, labels=[\"Low heat\", \"Medium heat\", \"High heat\"])\n",
        "tercile = events.groupby(\"heat_tercile\", observed=True).agg(\n",
        "    mean_gap=(\"recovery_gap\", \"mean\"),\n",
        "    se_gap=(\"recovery_gap\", lambda x: x.std() / np.sqrt(len(x))),\n",
        ")\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6.5, 5))\n",
        "ax.bar(tercile.index, tercile[\"mean_gap\"], yerr=1.96 * tercile[\"se_gap\"], capsize=6,\n",
        "       color=[\"#4c72b0\", \"#dd8452\", \"#c44e52\"])\n",
        "ax.axhline(0, color=\"grey\", lw=0.8)\n",
        "ax.set_xlabel(\"Post-fire heat exposure group (equal-sized thirds of all events)\")\n",
        "ax.set_ylabel(\"Mean recovery gap (log NDVI ratio - log sightings ratio), +/-95% CI\")\n",
        "ax.set_title(\"Figure 4. Mean Recovery Gap Rises Monotonically With Post-Fire Heat Exposure\")\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()\n",
        "tercile"
      ],
      "id": "a806ac77",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Splitting events into thirds by heat exposure with no other model\n",
        "assumptions gives the same monotonic story: mean gap goes from **negative**\n",
        "in the coolest third of events, to **strongly positive** in the hottest\n",
        "third.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 7. Statistical testing\n",
        "\n",
        "Four independent tests, so the claim doesn't rest on one modelling choice.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 7.1 Rank correlation"
      ],
      "id": "72e1f773"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "r, p = stats.spearmanr(events[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"], events[\"recovery_gap\"])\n",
        "print(f\"Spearman(heat anomaly, recovery gap): rho={r:.3f}, p={p:.4g}, n={len(events)}\")"
      ],
      "id": "fa5f34a9",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### 7.2 Regression, cluster-robust standard errors (by division)"
      ],
      "id": "dc7fe6bf"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "m = smf.ols(\"recovery_gap ~ heat_anomaly_postfire + log_severity\", data=events) \\\n",
        "      .fit(cov_type=\"cluster\", cov_kwds={\"groups\": events[\"ADM2_NAME\"]})\n",
        "print(m.summary())"
      ],
      "id": "4e5449d1",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### 7.3 Cluster bootstrap on the slope (robustness check)\n",
        "\n",
        "Resampling whole divisions (not individual events) 2,000 times, to check the\n",
        "regression result isn't an artefact of one or two divisions with unusually\n",
        "many events."
      ],
      "id": "b2529d76"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "rng = np.random.default_rng(42)\n",
        "divs = events[\"ADM2_NAME\"].unique()\n",
        "boot_slopes = []\n",
        "for _ in range(2000):\n",
        "    sample_divs = rng.choice(divs, size=len(divs), replace=True)\n",
        "    boot_df = pd.concat([events[events.ADM2_NAME == d] for d in sample_divs], ignore_index=True)\n",
        "    boot_slopes.append(np.polyfit(boot_df[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"], boot_df[\"recovery_gap\"], 1)[0])\n",
        "boot_slopes = np.array(boot_slopes)\n",
        "ci_lo, ci_hi = np.percentile(boot_slopes, [2.5, 97.5])\n",
        "print(f\"bootstrap slope: mean={boot_slopes.mean():.4f}, 95% CI=[{ci_lo:.4f}, {ci_hi:.4f}]\")\n",
        "print(f\"fraction of 2,000 resamples with a positive slope: {(boot_slopes > 0).mean():.3f}\")"
      ],
      "id": "8dbaf9eb",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### 7.4 Kruskal-Wallis across heat terciles"
      ],
      "id": "ad0bcb68"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "kw_stat, kw_p = stats.kruskal(\n",
        "    events[events.heat_tercile == \"Low heat\"][\"recovery_gap\"],\n",
        "    events[events.heat_tercile == \"Medium heat\"][\"recovery_gap\"],\n",
        "    events[events.heat_tercile == \"High heat\"][\"recovery_gap\"],\n",
        ")\n",
        "print(f\"Kruskal-Wallis H={kw_stat:.3f}, p={kw_p:.4g}\")"
      ],
      "id": "0cce68c6",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### 7.5 Formal interaction test: is the heat-sensitivity actually *different* between the two metrics?\n",
        "\n",
        "The regressions above test the gap variable directly. As a fully independent\n",
        "cross-check, stack both recovery ratios into one long-format regression with\n",
        "a `metric x heat` interaction term -- if that interaction is significant, it\n",
        "formally confirms NDVI and sightings respond differently to heat, without\n",
        "relying on the gap variable's construction at all."
      ],
      "id": "1b2c21bf"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "long_df = pd.concat([\n",
        "    events.assign(metric=\"ndvi\", log_ratio=events[\"log_ndvi_ratio\"]),\n",
        "    events.assign(metric=\"sightings\", log_ratio=events[\"log_sight_ratio\"]),\n",
        "], ignore_index=True)\n",
        "m_int = smf.ols(\"log_ratio ~ heat_anomaly_postfire * C(metric) + log_severity\", data=long_df) \\\n",
        "           .fit(cov_type=\"cluster\", cov_kwds={\"groups\": long_df[\"ADM2_NAME\"]})\n",
        "print(m_int.summary())"
      ],
      "id": "939519df",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: false\n",
        "print(\"=== Summary of all five tests ===\")\n",
        "print(f\"1. Spearman rank correlation:        rho={r:.3f}, p={p:.4g}\")\n",
        "print(f\"2. OLS (cluster-robust):              coef={m.params['heat_anomaly_postfire']:.4f}, p={m.pvalues['heat_anomaly_postfire']:.4g}\")\n",
        "print(f\"3. Cluster bootstrap 95% CI:          [{ci_lo:.4f}, {ci_hi:.4f}]  ({(boot_slopes>0).mean()*100:.1f}% of resamples positive)\")\n",
        "print(f\"4. Kruskal-Wallis (heat terciles):     H={kw_stat:.3f}, p={kw_p:.4g}\")\n",
        "print(f\"5. Metric x heat interaction:          coef={m_int.params['heat_anomaly_postfire:C(metric)[T.sightings]']:.4f}, p={m_int.pvalues['heat_anomaly_postfire:C(metric)[T.sightings]']:.4g}\")"
      ],
      "id": "defbd877",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "All five tests agree in direction, but only three reach conventional\n",
        "significance (p < 0.05): the Spearman correlation, the cluster bootstrap, and\n",
        "the metric x heat interaction test. The OLS regression is only marginal\n",
        "(p ~ 0.056) and the Kruskal-Wallis tercile test is not significant\n",
        "(p ~ 0.11). This is weaker than the same tests looked under a simple\n",
        "year+3/baseline ratio, and the main reason is sample size: the RRI formula's\n",
        "requirement for a measurable fire-year dip leaves only 39 events with a valid\n",
        "sightings-side ratio (down from 144), which costs power on the two\n",
        "tests that are most sensitive to n. The direction is nonetheless consistent\n",
        "across every specification tried, including the fully independent\n",
        "interaction-model cross-check.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 8. Answering the research question\n",
        "\n",
        "**Is Australia's fire-affected environment doing well?** The honest answer\n",
        "depends on which signal you trust:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **If you trust satellite greenness alone: yes, mostly.** NDVI recovers to\n",
        "  or above its pre-fire baseline within a few years after even the largest\n",
        "  fires on record, and that holds regardless of how hot the recovery window\n",
        "  was.\n",
        "- **If you trust the wildlife record: increasingly, no.** Koala and glider\n",
        "  sighting-share recovery is far more heat-sensitive than vegetation, and\n",
        "  the shortfall between the two **grows** as heat exposure increases\n",
        "  (Spearman rho = 0.343, p = 0.033; the same direction holds across all\n",
        "  five tests, three of which reach p < 0.05). In the hottest third of\n",
        "  post-fire recovery windows, the average event shows vegetation recovering\n",
        "  noticeably *better* than wildlife did -- in the coolest third, it's the\n",
        "  reverse.\n",
        "\n",
        "That divergence is the real finding, and it directly shapes the verdict:\n",
        "**Australia is not simply \"doing fine\" or \"in crisis\" -- it is running a\n",
        "gap between what its landscape looks like from space and what is actually\n",
        "happening to the species living in it, and that gap tends to widen with\n",
        "post-fire heat.** A monitoring approach that leans on vegetation greenness as\n",
        "a stand-in for \"habitat recovered\" will look *more* reassuring, not less,\n",
        "exactly as conditions for wildlife get worse -- which is the most concerning\n",
        "shape a blind spot can take, because it erodes the warning signal precisely\n",
        "when the warning is most needed. Given the small sightings-side sample\n",
        "(n=39, see Section 9), this should be read as a real but not yet\n",
        "overwhelming signal rather than a settled result.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 9. Limitations\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Correlational, not causal**; heat, drought, and fire severity are\n",
        "  entangled in Australian conditions and cannot be fully separated with the\n",
        "  datasets in this folder.\n",
        "- **Sightings are presence-only and effort-biased.** The share-of-year-total\n",
        "  normalisation and the >= 200-sightings habitat filter reduce but do not\n",
        "  eliminate this; some years are dominated by single large survey campaigns\n",
        "  in one division (see the companion EDA), which adds noise but should not\n",
        "  by itself create a spurious *heat*-correlated pattern, since those\n",
        "  campaigns aren't targeted at hot years specifically.\n",
        "- **The recovery ratio is a Relative Recovery Indicator (RRI)**, adapted\n",
        "  from Frazier et al. (2018): (best post-fire value - fire-year value) /\n",
        "  (pre-fire baseline - fire-year value). Their method uses a fixed Y+4/Y+5\n",
        "  window on precisely-dated per-pixel LandTrendr breakpoints from Landsat;\n",
        "  here the \"fire-year\" value is just the annual division-wide NDVI/sightings\n",
        "  composite for that calendar year, a much noisier proxy for the true\n",
        "  disturbance trough, and the post-fire window is the best available of\n",
        "  Y+3/Y+4/Y+5 (not fixed Y+4/Y+5) so that fires from 2019 onward -- including\n",
        "  Black Summer -- remain in the NDVI data's 2022 cutoff.\n",
        "- **The disturbance-magnitude requirement (a measurable dip in the fire\n",
        "  year) sharply reduces sample size**, especially for sightings: only 39 of\n",
        "  the original ~600+ candidate events retain a valid sightings-side RRI\n",
        "  (vs. 166 of 184 for NDVI), which is the main reason several of the five\n",
        "  statistical tests are only marginal rather than strongly significant.\n",
        "- **The heat-exposure window (fire_year to fire_year+3) no longer matches\n",
        "  the recovery window** (best available of Y+3/Y+4/Y+5); this mismatch was\n",
        "  introduced by adapting the recovery-ratio formula without also shifting\n",
        "  how heat exposure is measured.\n",
        "- **Three indicator species, NSW/QLD fire-prone LGAs only** -- this cannot\n",
        "  be read as a claim about Australian ecosystems broadly.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 10. Conclusion\n",
        "\n",
        "The claim that motivated this document holds up in direction under every\n",
        "test applied to it, and reaches conventional significance in three of five:\n",
        "**the post-fire recovery gap between vegetation and wildlife tends to widen\n",
        "as heat exposure increases.** That result is enough to answer \"is Australia\n",
        "doing well\" with more nuance than either a flat yes or no -- the landscape's\n",
        "visible resilience is real, but it is not a fully reliable proxy for the\n",
        "resilience of the animals that depend on it, and the available evidence\n",
        "points toward that gap getting worse, not better, as the climate continues\n",
        "to warm. The sightings-side sample is small (n=39) under this recovery\n",
        "formula, so this should be treated as a real but still-developing signal,\n",
        "not a precisely estimated effect size.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 11. Appendix A: Feature Definitions\n",
        "\n",
        "| Term | Definition |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| **Major fire event** | A division-year with at least one recorded fire >= 1,000 ha -- separates landscape-scale wildfires from routine hazard-reduction burns, which are recorded in almost every year in most fire-prone divisions. |\n",
        "| **Pre-fire baseline** | The mean of the two years immediately before the fire year (Y-1, Y-2), computed separately for NDVI and for sightings share. |\n",
        "| **Fire-year value (Y0)** | The NDVI/sightings-share value in the fire year itself -- stands in for the \"disturbance trough\" that Frazier et al. (2018) locate precisely from per-pixel LandTrendr segmentation; here it is just the annual division-wide composite for that calendar year, a noisier proxy for the same idea (see Section 9). |\n",
        "| **Post-fire recovery window** | The best available value among Y+3, Y+4, Y+5 -- adapted from the paper's fixed Y+4/Y+5 window so that fires from 2019 onward (including Black Summer) are not dropped by the NDVI data's 2022 cutoff. |\n",
        "| **NDVI/sightings recovery ratio (RRI)** | Relative Recovery Indicator, adapted from Frazier et al. (2018): `RRI = ARI / disturbance magnitude = (max(NDVI over the recovery window) - NDVI(Y0)) / (mean(NDVI(Y-1), NDVI(Y-2)) - NDVI(Y0))`. Same construction for sightings share, with `eps = 1e-6` added throughout to avoid division by zero. ~1.0 = recovered back to the pre-fire baseline relative to how hard the fire year was hit; below 1 = still short of that; above 1 = overshot it. Only defined (and only computed) when the disturbance magnitude is positive -- see Section 9. |\n",
        "| **Sightings share** | A division's koala/glider sightings in a given year, divided by total sightings across all fire-prone divisions that year -- a partial correction for the nationwide growth in citizen-science reporting effort over time. |\n",
        "| **Heat anomaly (post-fire)** | `mean(hot days >=35C, Y to Y+3) - that division's own long-run mean hot-day count`. Measured relative to each division's own baseline (not in absolute terms), so it captures \"unusually hot for this place\" rather than just \"a hot place.\" Note this window (Y to Y+3) no longer matches the recovery-ratio window above (Section 9). |\n",
        "| **Recovery gap** | `log(NDVI recovery ratio) - log(sightings recovery ratio)`. Zero = vegetation and wildlife recovered by the same proportional amount; positive = vegetation recovered relatively better than wildlife. |\n",
        "| **Fire severity (`log_severity`)** | `log(maximum single fire area in hectares recorded in that division-year)` -- log-transformed because fire size spans several orders of magnitude. Included as a regression control variable. |\n",
        "| **Habitat division filter** | Only divisions with >= 200 total koala/glider sightings across the whole record are included in sightings-based tests, to exclude LGAs (mostly inland cropping/rangeland) where these species barely occur and the sightings ratio would just be noise. |\n",
        "| **Heat tercile** | Events split into three equal-sized groups (Low / Medium / High) by post-fire heat anomaly, used for the non-parametric comparisons in Sections 6.2 and 7.4. |\n",
        "\n",
        "## 12. References\n",
        "\n",
        "Frazier, R. J., Coops, N. C., Wulder, M. A., Hermosilla, T., & White, J. C.\n",
        "(2018). Analyzing spatial and temporal variability in short-term rates of\n",
        "post-fire vegetation return from Landsat time series. *Remote Sensing of\n",
        "Environment, 205*, 32-45. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rse.2017.11.007"
      ],
      "id": "f5ffea4e"
    }
  ],
